WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.160
- So a bunch of Redditors
on WallStreetBets

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.400
found out that a hedge
fund was shorting GameStop.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.860
So they thought it'd be
fun to raise the price.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.540
In the process, they may have destroyed

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.720
the financial markets as we know them.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:11.860
Then the hedge fund says

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.740
that the Redditors are
manipulating the market.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.180
The Redditors say that the hedge fund

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.200
is manipulating the market.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.370
And then Robinhood says
that no one can even trade

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.860
and everyone loses a ton of money

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.300
and everyone's gonna
end up suing each other.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:26.517
But Hans, Bobi, I'm your white knight.

00:00:26.517 --> 00:00:28.730
- I must have missed 60 minutes.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.750
- And I'm here to
explain what in the world

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.350
is going on with GameStop,
Reddit, Robinhood

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.020
and the entire stock market.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.478
And whether any of them this is legal.

00:00:38.478 --> 00:00:40.830
(upbeat music)

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.340
Sponsored by Ting Mobile.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.940
Hey, LegalEagles, it's time to think

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.010
like a securities' lawyer

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.340
because trading on the stock
market is booming right now.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.100
According to online brokerages,
there's been a huge increase

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.830
in trading activity during the pandemic.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.490
And in particular, a ton of
people have been hanging out

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.890
on the subreddit forum WallStreetBets.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.710
Where they discuss stock tips information

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.774
while also making fun
stock memes, basically.

00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:05.273
- Stonks

00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:07.440
- And part and parcel to that.
Many people have signed up

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.110
with the trading app and
brokerage known as Robinhood

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.920
which says it's on a mission
to democratize finance for all.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.020
Now, Robinhood is particularly interesting

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.790
because unlike most
brokerages they encourage

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.410
or at least allow a huge number of people

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:25.210
to trade on margin or to
access options trading

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.490
which as we'll talk about in a few minutes

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.400
is sort of like giving
dynamite to children.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.960
Now, of course, that might
cause a huge amount of damage

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.380
but it's definitely
going to be fun to watch.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.000
And speaking of which, there
are a lot of gamers on Reddit

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.430
and they've taken an active interest

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:43.960
in the retail video game
shop known as GameStop.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.670
There are still 5,000
GameStop stores around America

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:48.930
but there's also some reason

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.600
to think that GameStop is going

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.990
to struggle in the near future.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.680
It faces challenges like
closing malls and competition

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.130
from illegal downloads
and also legal downloads.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.430
Since a lot of people
are downloading games

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.120
instead of purchasing the physical copies

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.840
in brick and mortar stores,

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.770
the company doesn't expect
to turn a profit until 2023.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.920
Some people think GameStop
may even disappear

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.640
just like Blockbuster,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.500
but not everyone agrees that
GameStop is on their last legs.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.150
And the people of Reddithave
been talking about GameStop

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.650
as a good company for a couple of years.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.740
Then some big time
investors announced that

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.440
they had invested in GameStop,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.450
including the co-founder of
the online pet store Chewy.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.760
This made some investors take notice

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.020
and Redditors realized that many investors

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.930
had taken a short position on GameStop.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.940
The people of this subreddit
decided that if they acted

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.610
in concert to drive up
the price of the stock

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.360
they could actually make a
good profit by squeezing people

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.660
who had shorted the GameStop stock,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.750
man, try saying that
a couple of times fast

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:48.990
but just a few days ago,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.190
there were 1.9 million
members of WallStreetBets.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.790
Now it's up to four,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.430
maybe 5 million members of WallStreetBets.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.900
So if a substantial percentage

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.870
of those people bought GameStop,

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.470
then they really could have
an impact on the stock.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.570
And that is exactly what we saw happen.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:11.389
In January Redditors got to
work buying up GameStop stock.

00:03:11.389 --> 00:03:14.030
GameStop's stock price was trading

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.210
at under $20 at the beginning of the year.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:22.180
But by January 27th,
it had soared to $350.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.370
Now the financial industry is up in arms.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.570
The SEC is watching,

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:28.530
Redditors are pushing up the
price of several other stocks,

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.790
including AMC, Elon Musk,

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.210
and the Winklevoss twins
are cheering them on.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.350
And so there is a bit
of a David and Goliath

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.020
framing in this whole war.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.820
Especially if you're reading Reddit.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.330
The Redditor investors are saying

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.220
that they're doing just what regular folks

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.270
When they invest in the markets

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:46.060
trying to bet on stock prices on the rise.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.150
So although the traditional
investor community

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.380
is somewhat to gas at all this activity,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.800
one could argue that
the little guys and gals

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.360
were just trying to do
what the wealthier people

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.310
have been doing for years,

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.500
hoping to make money
from a company's growth.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.130
Though the giant hedge funds,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.360
many of whom drive the
short stock positions

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.490
are pretty mad about this.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.010
And they are of course
the enemy of the people,

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.100
or at least that's according to Reddit.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.350
But before we grand stand

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.150
too much further down
the Victor Hugo track,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.980
keep in mind that there are
lots of institutional investors

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.860
like these hedge funds like
pension funds, endowments

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.910
and credit unions who may
suffer also huge losses

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.810
because of what these Redditors
are doing with GameStop

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.080
and the reverberations that are going

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:27.890
through the entire stock market,

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:30.350
which takes us to the crux
of what's going on here

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.420
because you can't understand
the GameStop controversy

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.880
without understanding the mechanics

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.880
of the trades that are going on.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.650
The question is who is
getting hurt by traders

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:42.400
trying to inflate the stock price.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.540
Well, it's the people who took
a short position on GameStop.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:46.670
And here's a quick explanation

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.410
of what that means to short a stock.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:51.370
People obviously invest in
the stock market to make money

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.510
and most small investors
trying to buy a stock

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.680
that has not yet reached
its full potential.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.270
Making the bet that something will happen

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.167
to increase the value of
that stock or security.

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:01.130
- Stonks.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.450
- But investors can also bet

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.400
against the company's future potential.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:05.700
This is called shorting a stock.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.190
Shorting a stock enables an investor

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.950
to actually make money
when the stock goes down.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.620
Because you can't make
money by buying a stock

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:12.540
hoping it goes down.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:13.710
Because then the value of the thing

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.930
that you have purchased has gone down.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.630
For an investor to short a stock

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.640
they actually have to borrow
the stock from someone else.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.150
The investor does this by
opening a margin account,

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.700
which lets them borrow the stock itself.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.170
The short seller wants to
see that the stock price

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.240
will fall in the future,

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:30.560
allowing the short seller to buy

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.940
the stock back at an even lower price

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.710
and pocketing the difference.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.080
But this is a riskier position

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.380
than simply buying a stock

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.380
with the hope that the value goes up.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.090
When you buy a stock, let's say $20.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.030
The most you can lose is
the amount you paid $20.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:45.950
And that will only happen

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:48.120
if the stock you bought goes to zero.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.790
But shorting a stock is dangerous because

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:53.630
at some point you must buy back
the stock that you borrowed.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:54.810
So if you're shorting the stock,

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.310
you borrow the stock at, let's say $20,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.930
and it skyrockets to 500
you're in a world of hurt.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.270
If the value of the stock goes up

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:04.330
there's no limit to how
much you might lose.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.410
The downside risk is literally infinite.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.770
You might have to buy
it back at a huge loss.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:11.520
In our example, the
short seller is squeezed.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.020
They need to pony up $500

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.550
on an initial investment of only $20.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:18.363
So you can do the math.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.480
- I'm in danger.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.670
- This appears to be what happened

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:22.760
with at Redditday traders

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.170
versus the institutional investors

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:26.720
who took a short position on GameStop.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:27.830
A lot of the people on Reddit

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:30.890
just simply like GameStop as a company.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:32.660
So they wanted to build up the price.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:35.490
And if they could destroy a
hedge fund at the same time,

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:36.550
so much the better.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.980
And at some point Redditors
on WallStreetBets realized

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:40.740
that more people were shorting the stock

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:41.650
than actually owned it.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:44.160
In fact, 140% of the float.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.260
A lot of people think that
this is illegal, it's not,

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.390
this is a relatively normal thing.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:51.730
And hedge fund Melvin Capital
was one of the hedge funds

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.070
who was on the other end of the trade.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:55.790
We know by now that Melvin capital

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:57.440
has closed out its short position

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.290
which means that they
had to buy all the stock

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.360
that they were shorting at
a much, much higher price.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.380
No one is been able to exactly
confirm how much they lost

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:07.930
but we do know that Citadel

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:11.540
and Point72 bigger hedge
funds have infused close

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:15.941
to $3 billion to Melvin capital
to shore up its finances.

00:07:15.941 --> 00:07:18.970
And Melvin capital only had
roughly $12 billion to start.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:20.500
And this has fueled speculation

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.620
that Melvin capital
might have gone bankrupt

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.400
if not for the cash infusion

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:25.760
but that's where things got even crazier.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.090
With the price of the GameStop
stock fluctuating wildly

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.580
going up thousands of percent,

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:32.850
in the middle of all of this,

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.710
Robinhood, the preferred
brokerage and trading app

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.140
of the people on WallStreetBets decided

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:42.350
to stop all trading on
GameStop on January 28th.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.480
And some have reported
that as many as half

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.490
of the people that use the
Robinhood app in general

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.650
had some sort of position on GameStop.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.130
And some have reported that

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:56.050
after Robinhood stopped
all trading on a GameStop

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:58.280
they allowed some people
to exit their positions

00:07:58.280 --> 00:07:59.540
but not buy back in.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:00.750
And also some report

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.940
that Robinhood was actually
closing their positions

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.870
and selling other options or
stock in either GameStop or AMC

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:09.950
causing fury among the people

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:12.610
that were trying to hold
onto the stock forever.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.300
So with that admittedly
superficial explanation

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.870
of the facts here.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:18.340
Now we get to the legal questions.

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:19.910
Is any of this illegal?

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:23.480
Is it illegal for a bunch
of people on the internet,

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:24.760
if not to band together

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.210
then at least express
what they're going to do

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.360
in the hopes that they drive
prices in a certain direction.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.520
Does the SEC care about that?

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.010
Is it illegal for
Robinhood to prevent people

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.260
from trading in the way that they want?

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:38.640
Is that something that can give rise

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.120
to a class action lawsuit?

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.670
And of course the initial action

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:43.810
that gave rise to all of this,

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:46.080
is it legal for the hedge funds to have

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.480
a massive short position and
try and drive the market?

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:53.820
And the answer as with
most things is, it depends.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.310
So let's first talk about
market manipulation.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.930
As you probably know,
securities fraud is illegal.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:00.560
Some common types of security fraud

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:03.590
include manipulating stock
prices, lying on SEC filings

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:05.160
and committing accounting fraud,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:06.630
but fraud also includes activity

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:08.280
that manipulates the market.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.210
So what do we mean by market manipulation?

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:11.830
And why does it matter?

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:13.700
Market manipulation tends to distort

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:15.710
the prices of stocks and securities,

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.360
which damages the market's integrity

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.230
because the conduct can lead
to other market participants

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.490
to believe that the market
is rigged or unfair.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:24.160
And of course it actually changes

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.690
the prices that people
pay for these things.

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:28.660
- Because our economy is rigged.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.210
- And after the financial
meltdown of 2007, 2008,

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:35.040
that belief was obviously
somewhat justified.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.930
And many people lost their
homes and life savings

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.900
while giant financial
institutions got bailed out

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:42.220
after going effectively bankrupt.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:43.610
Now, after the financial crisis,

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:45.960
Congress passed the Commodity Exchange Act

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.550
as a method of combating
fraud in financial markets.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:51.970
Congress added the CEA section 6(c)

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:54.800
which prohibits manipulative
and deceptive devices

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.300
and contrivances and adopted
a rule which prohibits

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:01.140
any manipulative device,
scheme or artifice to defraud.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.300
Now market manipulation has been illegal

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:04.430
for a very long time.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:07.240
I believe it was in place
in the original Securities

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:10.570
and Exchange Act of 1934 under Rule 9.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:13.100
But I'm gonna focus on the CEA definition

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.340
of market manipulation
because it's both more recent

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:18.170
and seems to have a reasonably clear test.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.920
Now whether there is market manipulation

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:23.200
under the CEA is decided
by a four-part test.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.950
That the accused had the ability
to influence market prices.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.840
Two that the accused
specifically intended to create

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:31.020
or effect a price or price trend

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:32.850
that does not reflect legitimate forces

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.410
of supply and demand.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.730
Three that the artificial prices exists,

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:39.730
and four that the accused
caused the artificial prices.

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:40.563
So the first question,

00:10:40.563 --> 00:10:43.720
did Redditors have the ability
to influence market prices?

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.530
Well, it seems clear
after a few days of this

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.570
that the answer is obviously yes.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:51.406
They succeeded in dramatically
raising the price of stock.

00:10:51.406 --> 00:10:54.580
Now, did they intend to
inflate the GameStop stock

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:55.780
in a way that was legitimate

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:59.160
and did not reflect the
forces of supply and demand?

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:01.400
Well, obviously you
can argue it both ways.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:02.360
Now on the one hand,

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:05.300
this might be an example of
classic market manipulation

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:07.160
known as a pump and dump scheme,

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.500
but it doesn't really fit exactly

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:10.860
in that mill you.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.890
The classic pump and dump
scheme fraudsters talk up stocks

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.280
claiming that they have
insight information

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:16.330
about something that will cause

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.180
a stock's value to skyrocket.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:20.640
This insight information
is actually just nonsense.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.190
And the goal is to entice buyers

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:23.800
to quickly buy a penny stocks.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:25.160
And after the buyers jump in,

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:26.820
the fraudsters sell their own shares,

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:27.970
making a big profit

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:30.520
but causing the price
to drop dramatically.

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:33.140
Of course, the new buyers
are left holding their hat

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:34.240
and lose their money.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:35.870
This used to happen through cold calls,

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:37.850
but in the modern era
chat rooms and forums

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:39.050
have been fertile grounds

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:42.730
for fraudsters illegally
giving false information

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.430
in the hope of driving up a stock price

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:46.850
so that they can sell for much less.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.130
Pump and dump schemes are clearly illegal

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:52.570
under several laws, such as
the Securities Act of 1933,

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.870
the Securities and Exchange Act of 1934

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:58.640
And 18 USC Section 371 at SEC.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.130
Now, if this activity
does fall under the rubric

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:02.580
of market manipulation,

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.070
it would qualify as what scholars

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:06.280
call open market manipulation

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:08.560
because this isn't happening
behind closed doors.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.970
This isn't happening
because of a cold call,

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:12.730
it's happening in plain sight.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:14.300
It's happening on the internet

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:18.010
And everyone effectively has
access to this information.

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:20.310
And the SEC argues that
market manipulation can

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:22.427
in fact happen through social media,

00:12:22.427 --> 00:12:24.330
"One way fraudsters may
explore social media

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:25.890
is to engage in market manipulation,

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.690
such as spreading false
and misleading information

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:30.400
About a company to affect
that stock share price.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.240
Wrongdoers may perpetrate
stock rumors on social media

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:34.907
as well as on online bulletin boards

00:12:34.907 --> 00:12:36.600
and internet chat rooms.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.670
The false or misleading rumors
may be positive or negative."

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.510
And the subreddit
WallStreetBets really seem

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:42.760
to be exploiting social media

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.940
to spur interest in lifting
the GameStop stock price

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:47.540
in order to manipulate the market.

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:49.970
Four months ago a post
appeared on WallStreetBets

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:52.160
with a subject, Bankrupting
Institutional Investors

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:54.510
for Dummies featuring GameStop.

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:56.830
There's been a perception
that the traders of Reddit

00:12:56.830 --> 00:12:58.910
are just internet trolls
out for a joy ride.

00:12:58.910 --> 00:12:59.960
And the title of the post

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:02.230
certainly reinforces that perception.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:03.840
You also interpret this post as evidence

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.690
that the Redditors wanted
to manipulate the market

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:07.140
for GameStop stock,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:09.420
and in an illegal way
to hurt big investors

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:10.800
who shorted the stock.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.520
On the other hand,
there's not much evidence

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:14.440
that the posters who used WallStreetBets

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:17.750
We're lying about GameStop
or creating false rumors

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:19.320
designed to pump up the stock's value

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.330
so that they could induce
people to buy the stock.

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:23.980
WallStreetBets also has a counter-argument

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.260
that there's evidence that
Redditors were being thoughtful

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:29.210
about their position not
intending to screw people over.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:31.420
The post isn't just about
bankrupting short sellers

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:32.700
that lays out many
rules reasons to believe

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:35.497
that GameStop will make
it as a going concern,

00:13:35.497 --> 00:13:37.200
"Well it turns out their
books are rock solid.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.090
So they offered their
creditors a new set of terms

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:42.020
to push back debt for two
years, 50% of them took it.

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:44.560
They have eliminated all
excess goodwill impairments.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.630
There are 300 billion net cash position

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:48.090
with significantly reduced inventory

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.910
which explains the sharp
reductions in Q1/2 sales

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:53.750
and massively cut SG&amp;A by 28%

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.550
in preparation for the console cycle."

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:57.330
And of course, many
things can be true at once

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:58.230
especially when you're dealing

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.500
with a group of potentially
thousands of people

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:02.880
who all have different motivations
for what they're doing.

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:05.120
Some traders shared apparently
legitimate information

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:07.170
about GameStop and talked about it.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:08.880
Their arguments appeared to be convincing

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:10.840
to lots of other traders.

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.830
They have a free speech, right?

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:14.130
To make these statements.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.680
So was that market manipulation?

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:18.270
Well, we have to talk about intent

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.860
because the SEC and other
enforcement agencies argue

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.640
that even legitimate
transactions are manipulative.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:26.440
If the trader intends to
manipulate the market.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:28.530
The government's position
is that an investor's intent

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:30.230
is enough to turn a legitimate transaction

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.080
into illegal market manipulation.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.040
And anyone who's watched
this channel before at all,

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:39.070
knows that the same actual
conduct can become illegal

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:40.860
based on the mens rea the mental state

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.430
that you have when you
conduct that action.

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:44.860
And of course the traders of Reddit seemed

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.040
to have a mix motive,

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:47.450
and there are lots of different people

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.250
acting for many different reasons.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:51.100
On the other hand, there's
almost no argument.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:54.160
This isn't a really weird
and interesting case

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:57.240
in part because you generally
don't have the telltale signs

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:58.970
of securities fraud.

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:00.240
This is not the classic case

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.610
where someone is talking up a stock,

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:03.760
getting other people to buy

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:06.880
so that they can sell at
a higher price themselves,

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.300
really, well, there're exceptions to this

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:10.660
it seems that most of the things

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:13.360
that people are talking
about on Reddit are true.

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:14.300
They are not false.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.940
They are talking about
real things in the world,

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:19.210
as well as what they intend to do.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:21.610
They're trying to get the entire
internet to buy the stock,

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:23.470
hold it, drive up the price,

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:25.200
screw over the hedge
funds who are shorting it

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.130
and potentially save a company

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:29.810
that a lot of people
have fond memories over.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:33.570
Though granted fraud is not
required for market manipulation

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:35.210
under the SEC's definition.

00:15:35.210 --> 00:15:38.110
So just because people have good motives

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.370
doesn't mean that their
intend doesn't qualify

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.520
as illegal market manipulation.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:43.640
And at the same time,

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.800
anytime you have a whole
bunch of people coordinating

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.760
to change the price of something

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:52.210
you have to ask he question,
is that an antitrust violation.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:54.790
Because the Sherman
Antitrust Act Section 1,

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:56.900
deals with setting market prices

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:58.740
and that applies to even individuals.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:02.410
It doesn't just require
the monopolies and cartels

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.130
are the ones that can't set market prices.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:08.020
When you engage in a
conspiracy to set a price,

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:11.630
you might be engaged in something illegal

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:13.170
under the Sherman Antitrust Act.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:15.480
But let's talk about who's
manipulating whom here

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.360
because one reason there's so much fury

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:20.190
over this situation is that
people feel like markets

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:23.480
are nothing but a fake game anyway.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:25.560
And there's certainly a
kernel of truth to this.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.430
Since everyone that
participates in the market

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.360
changes prices by their very participation

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:32.660
in the market itself.

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:33.930
And that changes the prices

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:37.240
of stocks and securities
to some small degree.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:39.100
But of course the market is regulated.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.750
And in other words, there are
laws that constrain traders

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:43.410
from crossing certain lines

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:44.910
and the Reddit investors claimed that

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:46.590
the hedge fund get to
do whatever they want

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:49.430
to manipulate the markets
crossing every line

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:52.340
that retail investors don't get to cross

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:54.750
and are getting punished because of trying

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:56.150
to use the same tools that

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.170
the hedge funds have been using for years.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.710
And a lot of people would
absolutely argue that

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:02.890
this is a question of under enforcement

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:05.150
of the SEC's rules rather
than over enforcement.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:08.040
That there's nothing that
would exempt the hedge funds

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.340
from the exact same
rules as everybody else.

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:13.090
It's just a question
of the SEC's resources.

00:17:13.090 --> 00:17:15.100
The SEC has been classically underfunded

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:17.350
and there's no doubt that
the actual definition

00:17:17.350 --> 00:17:19.460
is codified in the law
of market manipulation

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:21.270
is incredibly broad.

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:22.390
It's another question entirely

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:24.940
whether all of the activity that falls

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:28.170
under that definition should
be considered illegal or not

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:30.630
but there's nothing that
would exempt the hedge funds

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:34.030
from being punished
under those same rules.

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:37.070
If the SEC decided to
make that a priority.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:38.780
But that being said, there is no validity

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:42.380
to the idea that short-selling
alone is an illegal practice.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.680
Even if someone you
don't like is doing it.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.470
Short selling has existed
for hundreds of years

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.350
basically as long as there
has been stock markets.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:51.810
Short squeezes have existed,

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:53.610
gamma traps have existed.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:57.410
All of these are just tools
and in and of themselves

00:17:57.410 --> 00:17:59.700
don't qualify as market manipulation.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:00.533
But that being said,

00:18:00.533 --> 00:18:02.140
since we're talking about the hedge funds,

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:04.840
let's talk about the
allegations of collusion

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:07.940
between Robinhood and the
hedge funds themselves.

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:11.360
Many have speculated some
with more evidence than others

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:16.360
that Robinhood agreed to stop
trading on the GameStop stock

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:21.200
specifically as a
concession to hedge funds

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:24.380
that it does business with
in one way or another.

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:29.100
And these allegations seem
like more conspiracy theories,

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.350
than they are based in real life.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.480
That being said, it is also possible,

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:36.010
if not more probable that these actions

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:38.410
are related to legitimate
business concerns

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:42.480
rather than a conspiracy to
defraud Robinhood customers.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.960
For example, most people
are trading on margin,

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:46.690
which means that they're
not actually purchasing

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:49.470
individual stocks so much as
getting the rights to purchase

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.290
the stocks from Robinhood

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:54.790
and the market makers that
Robinhood interacts with.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:56.350
And because of that,

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:59.290
a lot of these transactions
don't clear for many days

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:00.910
after they're put in place

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:03.390
these are incredibly
complicated transactions

00:19:03.390 --> 00:19:05.780
that involve a number
of different companies.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:07.060
And in this instance,

00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:08.990
Robinhood number one,
was probably was worried

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:09.880
about its own liquidity,

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.900
given the incredible volatility going on

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.640
it would be able to fulfill these orders.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:15.870
Number two, it was probably worried

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:18.940
about the counterparty
risk of the companies

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:20.490
that it was dealing
with to make the markets

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:21.500
and fulfill these orders.

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:23.200
And number three was probably worried

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.210
about the actual ability of its customers

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:28.540
who are trading on extreme margins

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:29.730
to place these orders,

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.250
to actually fulfill the things

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:33.080
that they were putting in place.

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:35.400
So it's entirely possible that Robinhood

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:37.560
had legitimate business justifications

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.060
for limiting the trading
in the way that it did.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:41.290
Though, just because Robinhood

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:43.700
might have had some
legitimate business reasons

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:46.120
for undertaking these practices.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:47.530
You can absolutely understand

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:50.300
why users of Robinhood were furious,

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:51.950
that Robinhood prevented them

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:54.230
from buying more shares of GameStop,

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:56.260
while only allowing people to sell

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:57.760
their positions in GameStop,

00:19:57.760 --> 00:19:59.150
which apparently resulted

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.160
in the price of GameStop plummeting.

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:03.660
And at the same time, there
were even isolated reports

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.170
of individual users
who said that Robinhood

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:08.180
literally sold the stock

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:10.470
that they had purchased in GameStop.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:13.830
Though, I have to believe that
in those particular cases,

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:15.600
those users were trading on margin

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:17.350
and were probably facing a margin call

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:20.600
where in order to meet
the reserve requirements

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:22.020
for their margin account

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:25.820
the brokerage sells stock to
meet that reserve requirement.

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:28.890
That's fairly common when
people are trading on margin

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:32.260
and I'd be surprised if
Robinhood sold people's positions

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:33.820
who weren't subject to a margin call.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:36.100
But the bottom line is that users

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:38.470
of Robinhood are absolutely furious

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:41.290
often for a perfectly legitimate reason.

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:43.150
And so, because this is America,

00:20:43.150 --> 00:20:44.940
they did what Americans do

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:48.050
and they almost
immediately sued Robinhood.

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:51.310
The first lawsuit filed by
Robinhood user Brendan Nelson,

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:53.980
in the Southern District
of New York the SDNY,

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:55.430
alleges that Robinhood engaged

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:57.010
in unlawful market manipulation

00:20:57.010 --> 00:21:00.130
by removing GameStop
stock from its platform.

00:21:00.130 --> 00:21:03.510
And Nelson seeks to make
his suit a class action.

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.060
And there's another lawsuit filed

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:06.490
in the Northern District of Illinois

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:09.160
by Robinhood user Richard Joseph Gatz,

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:12.460
and says that Robinhood protected
institutional investments

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:14.520
at the expense of its retail customers,

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:17.760
the traders, the WallStreetBets traders.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.810
Gatz alleges that this
sudden halts resulted

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:23.070
in major losses because
prices were decreasing

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:25.440
and Gatz couldn't make any moves.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:27.640
So Gatz has represented himself.

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:28.990
And it seems like immediately

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.650
after Robinhood started doing
this people started clamoring

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:33.380
for a class action lawsuit.

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:36.300
And many people asked, "Can
Robinhood be allowed to screw

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:38.620
over its user base in this way?"

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:40.400
Well, I hate to be the bearer of bad news,

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:42.130
but it's very likely that these lawsuits

00:21:42.130 --> 00:21:43.700
are going to be dead on arrival

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:45.060
for a couple of different reasons.

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:48.310
Number one, when you sign
up for a Robinhood account

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:52.210
you are forced to sign up for
their terms and conditions.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:53.110
To trade on Robinhood,

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:54.750
you have to sign their customer agreement,

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:56.880
regardless of whether you don't read it.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:59.470
This is generally an enforceable contract.

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:02.210
So the first question is what
did all of the users agree to?

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:04.110
And one of the very first
things that you'll see

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.130
which is very, very bad news

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.840
for someone trying to file
a class action lawsuit,

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:12.540
is that everyone under section
38 of the user agreement,

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:14.350
agreed to arbitration

00:22:14.350 --> 00:22:17.310
and agreed to forgo a class action suit.

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:18.490
Arbitration as you probably know

00:22:18.490 --> 00:22:20.880
is a form of alternative
dispute resolution

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:23.150
for resolving disputes out of court.

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:24.560
Now, arbitration is often seen

00:22:24.560 --> 00:22:26.520
as a boogeyman within legal circles.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:29.220
People think that arbitration
just means a plaintiff

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.840
will never get anything
and that's simply not true.

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:33.890
It just means that things
will be adjudicated

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:36.050
in front of an arbitrator
instead of a court

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:38.260
But an arbitrator has the ability

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:40.020
to fashion almost any remedy.

00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:42.700
So theoretically you can
get exactly the same remedy

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:45.000
that you can get in a courtroom.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:47.620
In an arbitration they
have the power to do so.

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:49.670
And the big issue here is that

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:53.260
the arbitration clause
includes a class action waiver

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:54.850
which means that you're
not going to be able

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:57.390
to have a class action represent

00:22:57.390 --> 00:22:59.470
all of the users of Robinhood,

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:02.100
even if you were able to
meet all of the requirements

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:04.270
for a class action under rule 23

00:23:04.270 --> 00:23:08.540
which I am very, very skeptical
that a plaintiff's lawyer

00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:11.610
would be able to meet the
requirements that you need

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:13.870
to have a class action in the first place.

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:14.950
Though at the same time,

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:16.880
that can be both a sword and a shield.

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:18.690
We've seen many times where a company

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:21.200
has included a class action waiver,

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:23.910
thinking that by breaking up
every individual plaintiff

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:26.020
into their own arbitration case,

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:28.560
that's actually in their favor except

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:32.090
that when thousands of
individuals take them up on that

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:34.810
and have the company
pay for the arbitration,

00:23:34.810 --> 00:23:36.100
that's going on,

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:38.610
the company can get really screwed

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:40.730
because they'll end up
paying a huge amount of money

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:43.230
just to go forward in arbitration.

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:45.930
So sometimes arbitrations
are sometimes they're bad.

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:47.480
They're just a tool,

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:48.840
but in this particular case

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.300
it's going to result almost
certainly in the dismissal

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:54.360
of the class action suit that
was filed against Robinhood.

00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:56.770
But then we get to the even
more fundamental issue here

00:23:56.770 --> 00:24:00.420
which is what the user agreement
allows Robinhood to do.

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:03.183
Even if plaintiffs could get
around the arbitration clause

00:24:03.183 --> 00:24:05.490
their arguments would probably be doomed

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:07.390
by the other provisions of the contract

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:08.550
that all of the users signed.

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:10.880
For example, the
agreement allows Robinhood

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:14.010
to take into account market
volatility, section 13

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:15.530
market volatility, market orders,

00:24:15.530 --> 00:24:16.990
limited orders and queued orders.

00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:18.900
I understand that during periods

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:20.160
of high volume illiquidity,

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:22.600
fast movement or volatility
in the marketplace,

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:24.360
the execution price received may differ

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:26.740
from the quote provided
on entry of an order.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:28.940
I may receive partial
executions of an order

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:30.000
at different prices.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.190
Of course, Robinhood reserves
the right to restrict trading

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:34.240
or prohibited altogether.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:36.500
Section 16 restrictions on trading.

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:39.030
I understand that Robinhood
may in its discretion prohibit

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:41.000
or restrict the trading of securities

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:44.380
or the substitution of
securities in any of my accounts.

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:46.020
I understand that
Robinhood may at any time,

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:47.070
at its sole discretion

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:48.250
and without prior notice to me:

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:51.060
one prohibit or restrict
my access to use the app

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:53.100
or the website or related services,

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:54.560
and my ability to trade,

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:57.200
two, refuse to accept
any of my transactions,

00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:59.620
three, refuse to execute
any of my transactions

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:02.150
or four, terminate my account.

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:03.620
Now that says nothing about whether

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:06.460
this was a good business decision or not.

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:08.090
People are rightfully upset.

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:10.220
People are furious with the decisions

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:11.490
that Robinhood has made,

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:13.580
regardless of whether
Robinson had to do it,

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:16.730
whether it's colluding with
the hedge funds or not,

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:19.743
people are probably going
to leave Robinhood on us

00:25:19.743 --> 00:25:22.380
because they hate the way
that they have been treated.

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:24.640
This is only to say
that the user agreement

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:27.350
that Robinhood wrote and made
all of its customer sign,

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:29.580
appears to give them a lot of leeway.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.640
And I would not be surprised
if these lawsuits fail

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:36.210
simply because the customers
agreed to it in advance.

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:37.680
But if you don't like it,

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:39.230
you can do business with somebody else.

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:41.580
And maybe that's a good
idea as Matt Levine

